{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"managing-data-asset-access","skill_description":"Grants, requests, audits and removes access to data assets in an AWS data catalog (the Amazon SageMaker Catalog supported by Amazon DataZone) through catalog subscriptions over published listings. Covers requesting a subscription, approving or rejecting requests with a recorded decision, sharing a listing with a project, revoking one or many subscriptions, and confirming grant settlement. Triggers on: give another team access to a table, request access to a dataset, approve or reject an access request, share a listing with a project, who has access to this table, revoke access, bulk revoke, is the access actually gone, subscription grant status. Not for: IAM roles, policies or STS (use aws-iam), S3 bucket policies (use securing-s3-buckets), Clean Rooms permissions (use aws-cleanrooms), or writing catalog metadata, glossaries and forms, publishing listings, or retiring or deleting an asset that other teams are subscribed to and the order of operations that needs (use managing-data-catalog)\n\n\nServices: datazone\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-asset-access"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"ingesting-into-data-lake","skill_description":"Import data into the AWS data lake from S3 files, local uploads, JDBC databases (Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, RDS, Aurora), Amazon Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery, DynamoDB, or existing Glue catalog tables (migration). Default target is S3 Tables; standard Iceberg on a general purpose bucket is supported where S3 Tables is not adopted. Handles one-time loads, recurring pipelines, migrations. Triggers on: import data, load data, ingest, sync database, migrate table, move data to AWS, set up pipeline, ETL, pull from Snowflake, query BigQuery into S3, export DynamoDB, CTAS, convert to Iceberg. Do NOT use for setting up or troubleshooting Glue connections (use connecting-to-data-source), creating empty tables (use creating-data-lake-table), running queries (use querying-data-lake), finding tables by fuzzy name (use finding-data-lake-assets), catalog audit (use exploring-data-catalog), or SaaS platforms like Salesforce, ServiceNow, SAP, MongoDB, Kafka.\n\n\nServices: glue, s3, s3tables, athena, dynamodb\nTasks: deploy, migrate\nPersona: developer, data-engineer\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"ingesting-into-data-lake"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS docs on Forecast cross-account IAM role support for dataset imports."}
